-- title:
--   A polynomial-time $0.498$-approximation for Quantum Max-Cut
-- statement:
--   There exists one uniform polynomial-time quantum algorithm which, for every finite graph with nonnegative rational edge weights, prepares a state with expected Quantum Max-Cut energy at least $0.498=249/500$ times the unrestricted optimum:
--
--   $$E_G(A(G))\ge 0.498\,\mathrm{OPT}(G).$$
--
--   Here $H_G=\sum_{i<j}w_{ij}(I-\mathrm{SWAP}_{ij})$. The output is prepared from zero-initialized wires using a finite H/T/CNOT circuit generated by a polynomial-time classical controller. The graph occupies the first output wires and all ancillas are retained. The inequality holds against every normalized complex comparison state, including entangled states. Empty graphs and zero weights are included. The target is exact in this fixed gate model and does not prescribe a particular construction. It is the campaign's first open improvement beyond the accepted $0.25$ foundation; no proof of this target is claimed.
-- source:
--   Campaign existential target at exact alpha=249/500, using the immutable QuantumMaxCutApproximationModel. Historical numerical target: Gharibian and Parekh, Almost Optimal Classical Approximation Algorithms for a Quantum Generalization of Max-Cut, 2019, Theorem 6, https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.APPROX-RANDOM.2019.31. Reference overview: https://marwahaha.github.io/quantum-maxcut-reference/problems/qmc. The published numerical guarantee is motivation; the formal target includes exact finite-gate preparation and a genuine runtime certificate.

import Definitions.Def_QuantumMaxCutApproximationModel

theorem QuantumMaxCut.exists_0498_approximation :
    ∃ A : QuantumMaxCut.Algorithm,
      QuantumMaxCut.PolynomialTime A ∧
      QuantumMaxCut.ApproximationRatio A (0.498 : ℝ) := by sorry
